# optimization-tool

A Python optimization tool that connects pymoo evolutionary algorithms with
Cadence Virtuoso Maestro for analog IC design optimization, with **AI surrogate assistance** for accelerated convergence.

## Architecture
![alt text][def]
```
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│  optimization_tool (Python)          │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │  pymoo / Bayesian Algorithm    │  │
│  │  (GA/NSGA2/DE/PSO/CMAES/…)     │  │
│  └────────────┬───────────────────┘  │
│               │                      │
│  ┌────────────▼───────────────────┐  │
│  │  AI Surrogate ( GP/RF/GBRT)    │  │                 
│  │  └─ Cold Start → Predict →     │  │
│  │    → Verify → Learn (inc.)     │  │
│  └────────────┬───────────────────┘  │
│               │ SKILL commands       │
│  ┌────────────▼───────────────────┐  │
│  │  virtuoso-bridge (SSH tunnel)  │  │
│  └────────────┬───────────────────┘  │
└───────────────┼──────────────────────┘
                │ SSH
┌───────────────▼──────────────────────┐
│  Linux (Virtuoso + Maestro)          │
│  └─ Spectre simulation               │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘
```

## Prerequisites

- Python 3.9+
- `virtuoso-bridge` installed and configured (SSH tunnel to Virtuoso)
- Cadence Virtuoso with Maestro
- pymoo and its dependencies

## Dependencies

All runtime dependencies are declared in `pyproject.toml` and installed
automatically by `pip install -e .`. They are grouped below by purpose.

### Core (always required)
| Package | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `numpy` | Vector/matrix math, design variables & objectives |
| `pandas` | Reading/writing CSV history, specs, and model files |
| `pymoo` | Evolutionary / multi-objective optimizers (NSGA2, DE, PSO, CMAES…) |
| `scikit-learn` | Gaussian-process surrogate (`GaussianProcessRegressor`) and RF/GBRT models |
| `scipy` | Statistical helpers used by some surrogates (e.g. `median_abs_deviation`) |
| `joblib` | Serializing the **trained GP model** (`.joblib`) — kernel hyperparameters, scalers, and training data |
| `requests` | HTTP client for the optional remote AI API surrogate |

### AI / Bayesian optimization (optional)
| Package | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `scikit-optimize` (`skopt`) | Bayesian optimizers `bayes_gp` / `bayes_rf` / `bayes_mo` (GP + EI / ParEGO) |

### GUI mode (`--gui`, optional)
| Package | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `PySide6` | Qt desktop application (windows, dialogs, widgets) |
| `matplotlib` | Convergence / Pareto / AI-learning-curve plots (embedded in GUI and CLI plots) |

### External (not on PyPI)
| Package | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `virtuoso-bridge` | SSH tunnel to Cadence Virtuoso/Maestro. Installed from a local path, **not** from PyPI: `pip install -e "file:///C:/Users/DELL/virtuoso-bridge-lite-main"` |

> **Note on the trained GP model:** the AI surrogate persists the trained model
> as `ai_models/model_obj{i}.joblib` (the real model) plus a human-readable
> `model_obj{i}.csv` hyperparameter summary. `joblib` is therefore a hard
> dependency whenever `--use-ai` is enabled.

## Install

```powershell
cd C:\Users\DELL\optimization_tool

# Create virtual environment
python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\activate

# Install the external Virtuoso bridge (local path, not on PyPI)
pip install -e "file:///C:/Users/DELL/virtuoso-bridge-lite-main"

# Install this package + all declared dependencies (numpy, pandas, pymoo,
# scikit-learn, scipy, joblib, requests, scikit-optimize, PySide6, matplotlib)
pip install -e .

# Optional: GUI-only extras if you only run --gui
# pip install -e ".[gui]"
```

## Usage

### Quick start (dry-run, no real simulation)

```powershell
python -m optimization_tool --generations 10 --population 20
```

### Real run (with Virtuoso simulation)

```powershell
python -m optimization_tool --real-run --generations 50 --population 50
```

### With AI surrogate assistance

```powershell
python -m optimization_tool --real-run --use-ai --generations 50 --population 50
```

The AI surrogate learns from real simulations and replaces some evaluations
with predictions, reducing the number of costly Spectre runs.

### With project archiving

```powershell
python -m optimization_tool --project-dir my_opamp_opt --generations 50
```

This creates a timestamped archive under `my_opamp_opt/runs/` with full
configuration, per-generation history, and final results.

### List available algorithms

```powershell
python -m optimization_tool --list-algos
```

### GUI mode

```powershell
python -m optimization_tool --gui
```

Launches a PySide6 desktop application with Cadence-inspired UI for configuring
and running optimizations interactively, including AI surrogate settings and
project management.

### All CLI options

| Argument | Default | Description |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| `--generations N` | 10 | Number of optimization generations |
| `--population N` | 20 | Population size |
| `--algo NAME` | nsga2 | Algorithm name (see `--list-algos`) |
| `--dry-run` | true | Run without real simulation (default) |
| `--real-run` | | Run actual Virtuoso simulation |
| `--project-dir DIR` | none | Project directory for archiving runs |
| `--run-directory DIR` | . | Remote Virtuoso run directory |
| `--csv-filename FILE` | none | Use local CSV instead of downloading |
| `--download-dir DIR` | none | Local directory for downloaded CSV |
| `--seed N` | 1 | Random seed |
| `--no-plot` | | Disable convergence/Pareto plots |
| `--plot-dir DIR` | . | Directory to save plot images |
| `--list-algos` | | List available algorithms and exit |
| `--quiet` | | Suppress verbose output |
| `--debug-libs` | | Also show DEBUG logs from third-party libraries (virtuoso_bridge, paramiko, urllib3, ...). Off by default so the console stays focused on optimization progress. |
| `--gui` | | Launch PySide6 GUI application |

> **Logging:** By default the root logger is set to `WARNING` and only the
> project's own modules (`optimization_tool`, `dclaw.surrogate`) emit `DEBUG`.
> Third-party libraries are pinned to `WARNING` to avoid flooding the console.
> Pass `--debug-libs` to also see their `DEBUG` output (useful while debugging
> the bridge or other dependencies). The GUI applies the same strategy.
| `--use-ai` | | Enable AI surrogate assistance |
| `--ai-type TYPE` | local_gp | AI backend: local_gp, random_forest, gradient_boosting |
| `--ai-csv FILE` | none | Historical CSV data for initial AI training |

## Available Algorithms

| Category | Algorithms |
|----------|-----------|
| Single-objective | ga, de, pso, cmaes |
| Multi-objective (2-3) | nsga2, spea2, nsga3, moead, rnsga3 |
| Many-objective (4+) | nsga3, moead, ctaea |
| Bayesian (single) | bayes_gp, bayes_rf |
| Bayesian (multi) | bayes_mo |

## AI Surrogate Assistance

The AI surrogate module accelerates optimization by replacing costly Spectre
simulations with fast model predictions. It supports three phases:

### 1. Cold Start (`cold_start_n`)
The first N evaluations use **real simulation** to collect initial training data.
Set to 0 to skip (e.g., when loading historical data).

### 2. AI Prediction
When the model's uncertainty is **below threshold**, the AI predicts objective
values directly — no simulation needed.

### 3. Real Verification
When uncertainty **exceeds threshold**, a real simulation runs and the result
is fed back to improve the model (**online learning**).

### Supported Models

| Model | Type | Best For |
|-------|------|----------|
| `local_gp` | Gaussian Process (RBF/Matern kernel) | Smooth response surfaces, small samples |
| `random_forest` | Random Forest regression | Robust to outliers, medium samples |
| `gradient_boosting` | Gradient Boosting + quantile | Non-smooth responses, large samples |

### Model Persistence (Save/Load)

After an optimization completes, the AI model's training data is automatically
saved to the project run directory,for example:

```
{project_dir}/nsga2/ai_models/
├── metadata.json          # Variable names + obj. specs
├── model_obj0.csv         # Model for objective 0 (Gain)
└── model_obj1.csv         # Model for objective 1 (PM)
```

On the next run, the system **automatically detects** and loads this data:

- **Variables matched by name** → columns are reordered automatically
  (handles variable order changes)
- **Same variable set required** → different circuit topology is rejected
  with a warning
- **Cold start skipped** → `cold_start_n` is set to 0 automatically
- **Incremental learning** → new data is appended to existing history

Use the **"启用历史模型继承"** checkbox in the GUI to enable/disable, or
the **"清零历史模型"** button to delete saved data.

> **Note:** Historical models are only valid when the circuit topology and
> objectives are the same. Variable order changes are handled automatically,
> but different variable sets will be rejected.

## Project Structure (with `--project-dir`)

```
my_project/
├── project.json              # Project metadata (auto-generated)
├── runs/
│   ├── 20260817_143001/      # Each run = timestamped directory
│   │   ├── config.json       # Full configuration snapshot
│   │   ├── variables.csv     # Variable names + bounds
│   │   ├── specs.csv         # Objective expressions + weights
│   │   ├── history.jsonl     # Per-generation log (JSONL)
│   │   ├── results.json      # Final Pareto set
│   │   ├── convergence.png   # Convergence plot
│   │   ├── pareto.png        # Pareto front plot
│   │   └── ai_models/        # ← NEW: AI training data (save/load)
│   │       ├── metadata.json
│   │       ├── model_obj0.csv
│   │       └── model_obj1.csv
│   └── ...
├── ai_models/                # ← Project-level AI data (legacy runs)
├── project_all_results.csv   # Consolidated results across runs
├── project_all_history.csv   # Consolidated history across runs
└── context/                  # Human notes (optional)
```

